<h1>Exercise Name:Russian Twists (Bodyweight Russian Twists).</h1>
<u><b><h4>Video Overview:</h4></b></u> 
<pre>The 3D anatomical animation shows a model seated on an exercise mat, leaning back slightly with knees
 bent and feet resting on the ground. The upper body rotates side-to-side while hands are clasped together,
 engaging both the front abs (rectus abdominis) and side waist muscles (obliques), which are highlighted
  in red and yellow to show target muscle activity during rotational movements.

</div>
</div>
<pre>
<h1> Step-by-Step How to Perform
</h1>
<u><b><h4></h4>1.Starting Position:</h4></b></u> Sit on your mat with your knees bent and feet flat on the floor. Lean your torso back at roughly a 45-degree angle to form a "V" shape with your thighs, keeping your back straight and chest lifted. Clasp your hands together in front of your chest.
<u><b><h4></h4>2.Engage the Core: </h4></b></u>Pull your belly button in toward your spine to stabilize your lower back before rotating.
<u><b><h4></h4>3.Twist to the Side: </h4></b></u>Exhale as you rotate your torso fully to the right, bringing your hands toward the floor beside your right hip.
<u><b><h4></h4>4.Twist to the Opposite Side:</h4></b></u> Inhale as you return to center, then exhale while rotating your torso to the left, bringing your hands toward your left hip.
<u><b><h4></h4>5.Continuous Rhythm: </h4></b></u>Continue twisting back and forth in a smooth, controlled motion without rushing or using momentum.



<h1>Key Benefits</h1>>

<b>◉Rotational Core Strength:</b> Targets and strengthens the internal and external obliques , which are crucial for twisting and rotational movements in sports and daily activities.
<b>◉Full Core Activation: </b>Simultaneously engages the upper, lower, and deep stabilization muscles of the abdominal wall.
<b>◉Enhanced Balance & Stability:</b> Improves trunk stability and posture by training your core to control dynamic rotational forces.
<b>◉Versatile Movement: </b>Easy to scale—can be done with feet flat on the floor (easier), feet elevated in the air (harder), or holding a weight/medicine ball (advanced).



<h1> Reps, Sets & Recommendations</h1>

Count1 rep every time you complete a twist to both sides (Left + Right = 1 Rep).

| Level | Reps Per Set | Sets | Rest Period |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
|Beginner | 10 – 12 reps (Feet on ground) | 3 Sets | 45 – 60 seconds |
|Intermediate | 15 – 20 reps (Feet slightly elevated) | 3 – 4 Sets | 45 seconds |
|Advanced | 20 – 25+ reps (Elevated or Weighted) | 4 Sets | 30 – 45 seconds |

>Pro Tip: Focus on twisting from your torso/shoulders rather than just moving your arms side to side. Keep your chest high and avoid rounding your spine to protect your lower back.
